   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">DXA-Derived Body Composition Across the Mexican National Football Pathway by Sex</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">DXA-Derived Body Composition Across the Mexican National Football Pathway by Sex</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Age Category</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Age Category</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and Playing Position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and Playing Position</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Soccer; lean soft tissue; bone mineral content; adiposity; elite athletes; body composition; dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Background/Objectives: Sex- and category-specific body-composition references are scarce for Latin American and female football players. We used dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Background/Objectives: Sex- and category-specific body-composition references are scarce for Latin American and female football players. We used dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">which quantifies lean soft tissue</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">which quantifies lean soft tissue</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">fat</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">fat</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and bone mineral content (BMC) regionally in one low-dose scan</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and bone mineral content (BMC) regionally in one low-dose scan</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">to describe body composition across Mexican national squads and to explore associations with playing position. Methods: We analyzed 272 DXA records acquired in 2026 under one standardized protocol (females: n = 133</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">to describe body composition across Mexican national squads and to explore associations with playing position. Methods: We analyzed 272 DXA records acquired in 2026 under one standardized protocol (females: n = 133</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">under-15 [U15] to senior; males: n = 139</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">under-15 [U15] to senior; males: n = 139</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">under-14 [U14] to senior) using 95% confidence intervals and height-adjusted</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">under-14 [U14] to senior) using 95% confidence intervals and height-adjusted</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">sex-stratified linear models with heteroscedasticity-consistent (HC3) standard errors and Holm correction. Results: Senior squads had 8.83 kg (females) and 13.23 kg (males) more total lean mass than the youngest category (Hedgesâ€™ g \textgreater  2) and 6.32 and 2.59 percentage points less body fat. Male fat mass was similar across categories (10.4--11.7 kg)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">sex-stratified linear models with heteroscedasticity-consistent (HC3) standard errors and Holm correction. Results: Senior squads had 8.83 kg (females) and 13.23 kg (males) more total lean mass than the youngest category (Hedgesâ€™ g \textgreater  2) and 6.32 and 2.59 percentage points less body fat. Male fat mass was similar across categories (10.4--11.7 kg)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">suggesting that the lower body-fat percentage of older male squads reflected greater lean mass; in females</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">suggesting that the lower body-fat percentage of older male squads reflected greater lean mass; in females</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">it reflected both greater lean mass and lower fat mass. Category remained associated with lean mass and BMC after height adjustment (Holm-adjusted p \textless  0.001) but was not significantly associated with male body-fat percentage (p = 0.118). No association between position and body composition was observed in females; male forwards had more lean mass and less fat than goalkeepers. Conclusions: These preliminary cross-sectional references allow staff to benchmark players against their own squad and to flag atypical lean or bone values for multidisciplinary review; they are not developmental trajectories or body-composition targets.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">it reflected both greater lean mass and lower fat mass. Category remained associated with lean mass and BMC after height adjustment (Holm-adjusted p \textless  0.001) but was not significantly associated with male body-fat percentage (p = 0.118). No association between position and body composition was observed in females; male forwards had more lean mass and less fat than goalkeepers. Conclusions: These preliminary cross-sectional references allow staff to benchmark players against their own squad and to flag atypical lean or bone values for multidisciplinary review; they are not developmental trajectories or body-composition targets.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
